response:
 The measure of each internal angle of a regular pentagon is given by the formula \(\frac{(5-2) \times 180^\circ}{5} = 108^\circ\). When the sides of the pentagon are extended to form a star, the angles at the vertices of the star (i.e., the angles inside the pentagon) will still be \(108^\circ\). The angle \(A\) in the star is equal to the arc measure between two non-adjacent vertices of the pentagon minus \(180^\circ\) (since it's an internal angle formed by extending the sides of the pentagon). The measure of angle \(A\) is the same as the internal angle of the pentagon, which is \(108^\circ\).

So, the measure of angle \(A\) is \(108^\circ\).

